For each alternative work in two steps.
Step 1, predictions: name the arms, controls, comparators and measured variables of the registered design (copy their names), and write what the HYPOTHESIS predicts and what the ALTERNATIVE predicts for the comparison between them.
Step 2, decide, for a POSITIVE result (d_positive) and for a NULL result (d_negative):
- eliminated: in Step 1 there is at least one named arm, control, comparator or measurement for which the two predictions DIFFER, and this result is the one the alternative did not predict. This is the usual case when the design has a control that receives the alternative's cause too: a placebo, sham or attention-matched control eliminates "expectation or attention alone explains the benefit" on a positive result; a control receiving the same co-intervention eliminates "the co-intervention explains it"; a measured mediator that does not move eliminates "the effect runs through that mediator".
- weakened: no named arm or measurement separates them, but the design measures something the alternative depends on, or makes it less likely.
- unchanged: the result is equally expected under the alternative.
Do not lower "eliminated" to "weakened" because a better experiment could separate them more cleanly: judge the design as registered. A design label alone ("randomised", "double-blind") counts only when it is the feature that separates the two (blinding does separate "the participants' expectation explains it").
For every "eliminated", give separating_feature: a phrase copied VERBATIM from the registered text above (the arm, control or measurement of Step 1). If you cannot copy such a phrase, the answer is not "eliminated".
An alternative cannot be eliminated by both results.

Reply with a single JSON object and nothing else:
{"alternatives": [{"id": "p1", "hypothesis_predicts": "...", "alternative_predicts": "...", "d_positive": "eliminated | weakened | unchanged", "d_negative": "eliminated | weakened | unchanged", "separating_feature": "verbatim phrase or null"}]}
